<section xml:id="topic-3-2-procedures">
  <time minutes="135" />
  <title>Procedures (Functions)</title>
 
  <introduction>
   <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="procedures,function-call-defn, arguments,procedural-abstraction,
      LOs-3-2, vocab-3-2"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      Our programs are getting longer and longer! Advanced programmers break up 
      large programs into smaller, more manageable pieces called 
      <term>procedures</term> or <term>functions</term>. This allows us to
       solve complex problems by breaking them into smaller subproblems and to reuse code.
      In this lesson, we will learn to write our own Python functions and learn how procedural abstraction 
      helps manage complexity.
    </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit3images/decomposition.png" alt="Problem decomposition" width="70%"/>

  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="procedures">
    <title>What is a Procedure or Function</title>
    <idx>procedures</idx>
    <idx>functions</idx>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.1</title>
      <p>
        A <term>procedure</term> is a named group of programming instructions that performs a task.
        Using procedures helps programmers organize code, avoid unnecessary code, and
        make programs easier to read, test, and update. Procedures may also be called
        <term>functions</term> or <term>methods</term> depending on the programming language.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      A <term>procedure</term> or <term>function</term> is a named block of code that performs a 
      task when it is called.    
    </p>

    <p>We have already used several built-in Python functions in our programs, for example 
     <c>input()</c> and <c>print()</c> which are input/output functions built into Python. Notice
     that functions end with parentheses () which can contain <term>arguments</term>, the data that 
     the function needs to do its job, for example what to print for the <c>print("Print this!")</c> function. 
     Some functions like <c>input()</c> or <c>random.randint()</c> also return values back like
     the user's input or a random number that we usually store in a variable.  A function is like a black box
      where you know what goes in and what comes out, but not necessarily how it works inside. 
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
  # The print() function takes 1 argument that it prints to the screen
  print("Hello World!")  

  # The input() function takes 1 argument, the prompt, and returns the user's input
  #     which we save in a variable
  name = input("Enter your name: ")

  # The random.randint() function takes 2 arguments, the low and high values, 
  # and returns a random number between those values (inclusive)
  random_number = random.randint(1, 10)
      </code>
    </program>

    <image source="_static/Unit3images/function.png" alt="Function with arguments and return value" width="65%"/>

     <idx><h>unplugged</h><h>functions</h></idx>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>unplugged functions</h></idx>
       <idx><h>unplugged</h><h>procedures</h></idx>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>unplugged procedures</h></idx>
      <idx><h>POGIL group activity</h><h>functions</h></idx>
        <idx><h>POGIL group activity</h><h>procedures</h></idx>

    <project label="unplugged-functions">
      <title>Unplugged: Functions</title>
      <statement>
     <p>
      In groups, choose one person to be the function. The person playing
      the function should choose a secret math formula with a variable and 2 operations,
      for example <c>2*x + 1</c>. Don't tell the rest of the group what the formula is! 
     </p>
     <p>
      The other members of the group will give the
      function different argument values for <c>x</c>, and the function will
      return the result of the formula. Write down the input/output pairs for each argument 
      and return value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, if the function's formula is <c>2*x + 1</c> and a group
      member provides <c>3</c> as the argument, the function returns <c>7</c>.
      After each member gets a return value, the group should try to figure
      out the function's secret formula. Then, discuss and answer the following 
      questions as a group.
    </p>
      <ol>
        <li>What arguments did your group provide to the function? 
        What return value did the function produce for each argument?
          </li>
        <li>What was the function's secret formula? 
          How did your group use the input/output pairs to try to figure it out?</li>
         <li>How does this activity demonstrate that a function can be reused with different arguments?</li>
         <li>Can you use a function without knowing how it works inside? 
          How does this activity demonstrate that a function is an abstraction like a "black box"?</li>
      </ol>
  </statement>
    <response/>
    </project>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="function-call-defn">
    <title>Procedure/Function Call and Definition</title>

  
<activity label="repeatedcode-this-old-man">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on all the lines that are completely identical and repeated.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>Look for lines of Python code that are completely identical.</p>
  </feedback>

  <areas>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played one.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my thumb.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played two.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my shoe.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played three.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my knee.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
  </areas>
</activity>

 <note>
      <title>3.2.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Calling a procedure runs a named sequence of steps, which helps programs reuse
        code and keep the overall program easier to read and follow.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.2</title>
      <p>
        A procedure is called by using its name in a statement so the program runs the
        steps stored in that procedure. When the call happens, the program pauses the
        current sequence of statements, executes the procedure's instructions, and then
        resumes at the next statement after the call.
      </p>
    </note>

<activity label="codelensSong1">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on the next button below the code to step through the code.
      Watch the red arrow as the program calls the <c>chorus()</c> function
      and then returns to the line where the function was called.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <program interactive="codelens" language="python">
    <code>
def chorus():
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")


print("This old man, he played one.")
print("He played knick-knack on my thumb.")
chorus()

print("This old man, he played two.")
print("He played knick-knack on my shoe.")
chorus()
    </code>
  </program>
</activity>

    

      <note>
      <title>3.2.A.6</title>
      <p>
        A procedure is defined with a name and a list of zero or more parameters. When
        the following code runs, a procedure named <c>pName</c> is defined with zero or
        more parameters (<c>p1, p2, ...</c>). When the procedure is called, the statements
        in <c>&lt;block of statements&gt;</c> execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
procedure pName(p1, p2, ...)
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}</pre>
    </note>

  <note>
      <title>3.2.A.2</title>
      <p>
        If a procedure does not need any input information to complete its intended task,
        it can be defined with its name followed by parentheses and a block of statements.
        Each time the procedure is called, the statements in the procedure execute, and
        then the program continues with the next statement after the call.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure may not need parameters when it always performs the same steps with
        no changing input (for example, displaying an instructions screen or resetting a
        game to a default starting state). Procedures need parameters when they need
        input information to complete the intended task. If a procedure should work for
        many different inputs (for example, greeting different users or calculating totals
        for different numbers), parameters make it flexible and reusable because the
        program can pass different arguments each time it is called. Using parameters
        when input varies helps avoid duplicating code and keeps the program easier to
        update.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Procedures with parameters make it easier to adapt an app's behavior or output
        to different users and situations without rewriting code. A single procedure with
        parameters can generate many clear, personalized messages for different users by
        using different argument values each time it is called.
      </p>
    </note>

  

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Some procedures use a return statement to send a result back to the part of the
        program that called the procedure, which allows the returned value to be stored in
        a variable, used in an expression, or displayed as output. The return statement
        ends the procedure immediately, even if more statements appear later in the
        procedure. When the following code runs in the body of a procedure, the value of
        <c>expression</c> is returned to the place in the program where the procedure was called.
      </p>
      <pre>
return expression</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Some procedures perform an action, such as displaying output or updating
        variables, and do not return a value. Other procedures compute a value and use
        a return statement so the result can be used elsewhere in the program.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Defining a procedure creates the named steps, but the code inside runs only when
        the procedure is called.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="arguments">
    <title>Arguments and Parameters</title>

   <note>
      <title>3.2.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Procedures can use parameters to receive input. A <term>parameter</term> is a variable name
        listed in the procedure definition. An <term>argument</term> is the actual value provided
        when the procedure is called.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.3</title>
      <p>
        In a procedure call, the values inside the parentheses are arguments. When the
        call runs, each argument value is assigned to the matching parameter in the
        procedure definition. This allows the same procedure to run with different input
        values. When the following code runs, <c>a1</c> is assigned to the first parameter
        and <c>a2</c> is assigned to the second parameter before the procedure's statements
        execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
pName(a1, a2)</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure can also be called with no arguments (empty parentheses) when it has
        no parameters. The number of arguments in a call must match the number of
        parameters in the procedure definition.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.5</title>
      <p>
        To determine the result of a procedure call, programmers trace the call by
        matching arguments to parameters, stepping through the procedure's statements in
        order, tracking any variable updates and outputs, and then returning to where the
        procedure was called to continue program execution.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.6</title>
      <p>
        If a procedure returns a value, the procedure call evaluates to that value. The
        returned value can be stored in a variable, used in an expression, or displayed
        as output. If a procedure does not return a value, the call is used to perform
        actions such as updating variable values or producing output.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="procedural-abstraction">
    <title>Procedural Abstraction</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction manages complexity by grouping related code into named
        procedures that can be called from other parts of a program.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.2</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction allows programmers to use a procedure by knowing what it
        does without needing to know how it works internally. Programmers often use
        built-in procedures, such as <c>print</c> or <c>random</c>, by relying on the
        procedure's name, purpose, and expected behavior while the implementation details
        stay hidden.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction helps manage complexity by breaking a large problem into
        smaller subproblems and creating procedures to solve each part.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.4</title>
      <p>
        The subdivision of a program into smaller, independent procedures or components
        is called modularity. Modularity helps programmers focus on one part at a time
        and makes programs easier to build, test, and revise.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.5</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction supports code reuse by extracting shared behavior into a
        single procedure instead of duplicating code in multiple places.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.6</title>
      <p>
        Using parameters generalizes a procedure so it can be reused with different
        inputs, allowing one procedure to handle many situations without rewriting code.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.7</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction allows programmers to change a procedure's internal steps
        (for example, to improve efficiency or fix a bug) without changing the rest of
        the program. This makes larger programs easier to maintain because updates can be
        made in one place.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.8</title>
      <p>
        Using procedural abstraction improves readability because the rest of the program
        can call procedures with descriptive names, so the overall steps of the program
        are easier to understand at a glance.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-3-2">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.A</title>
      <p>
        Develop code to define procedures with and without parameters.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.B</title>
      <p>
        Develop code for procedure calls, and determine the result of procedure calls.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.C</title>
      <p>
        Explain how the use of procedural abstraction manages complexity in a program.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

